    20	
    21	Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) studies have been a major step forward in characterizing macroscale neural dysfunctions related to affective symptoms in psychiatric patients (5,10,11). The emotional episodic memory task (12 -14), a widely used paradigm, effectively captures functional activation associated with adaptive memory-enhancing effects induced by emotional stimuli (15 -17). Maladaptive alterations in this task have frequently been observed in major depressive disorder (MDD) (18,19), bipolar disorder (BD) (20,21), and schizophrenia (SZ) (22,23), suggesting the neural dysfunctions of affective perturbation. Disrupted emotional memory retrieval has been reported that may manifest in multiple processes of pathological emotion in psychiatric patients, involving disturbances in emotional attention (24), perceptions (25), and regulation (26). Importantly, these disruptions were thought to link to the vulnerability and development of speci /uniFB01 c clinical affective syndromes (27,28), underscoring the task ' s relevance in assessing affective symptoms.
    22	
    23	ª 2025 THE AUTHORS. Published by Elsevier Inc on behalf of the Society of Biological Psychiatry. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/). 1
    24	
    25	Studies using emotional episodic memory tasks have identi/uniFB01 ed altered neurofunctions related to affective symptoms in the amygdala and medial temporal lobe (MTL) system across various mental disorders (29 -31), suggesting heterogeneous patterns of neural dysfunctions in distinct diagnoses (18,32 -34). However, inconsistent results have often been reported, both within one speci /uniFB01 c diagnosis and across disorders. In MDD, increased activity in the amygdala and orbitofrontal gyrus has often been associated with emotional bias (18,19), but another study has shown no abnormalities (33). Hypoactivation in the amygdala and occipital gyrus has been recognized in patients with BD compared with patients with MDD (20,35), although an earlier study reported increased activity (36). Individuals with SZ have shown both hyperactivation and hypoactivation in the prefrontal and parietal cortices (37 -39), accompanied by coactivation with the MTL areas (37,38,40). Furthermore, while a study has reported higher activation of the cingulate cortex in patients with BD relative to patients with SZ (34), others have reported the opposite (41).
    26	
    27	Although these mixed /uniFB01 ndings could be partially due to differences in task design, one more major reason is the individual differences within and across clinical cohorts, which were often not captured by traditional case-control designs (42,43). To address this, an individualized approach that disentangles the intersubject heterogeneity of neural dysfunctions that underpin affective symptoms is therefore necessary. Normative modeling quanti /uniFB01 es each patient as an extreme deviation from normative expectations for MRI-derived phenotypes based on clinical or cognitive characteristics by incorporating neural alterations into a continuous spectrum derived from a large healthy population (44,45). This method shares similarities with other dimensional methods commonly used to predict clinical variables from neuroimaging data (46). By cutting across diagnostic classi /uniFB01 cations, this approach shifts the focus from group-level analyses to individual-level deviations, thereby mitigating confounds arising from intersubject differences to some extent.
    28	
    29	Complex clinical phenotypes of affective symptoms are not only associated with macroscale functional abnormalities but are also tied to alterations at the microscale genetic and cellular levels (47 -49). Studies of postmortem brain samples and animal models have consistently pointed toward cellular processes in the central nervous system, including pathological alterations of neurons and neuroglial cells, as important factors in the development of affective symptoms (50 -52). However, due to limitations in spatial resolution and coarse diagnostic classi /uniFB01 cations, the relationship between microscopic vulnerabilities, macroscale dysfunctions, and clinical phenotypes remains poorly understood. The Allen Human Brain Atlas (AHBA) microarray dataset has been utilized to investigate transcriptomic patterns linked to neuroimaging /uniFB01 ndings (53,54), with substantial evidence indicating a connection between gene expression and macroscale dysfunctions (55,56). The brainwide gene expression atlas has made it possible to bridge the gap between cross-scale functional alterations (57) and, by combining with a normative modeling framework, could identify the personalized multiscale associations of neural vulnerability.
    30	

